 "label": "In English, the word ' so' when it opens a purpose clause right after an action and its object, with no comma before it ('hid the key so they couldn't find it', 'so that we could'); it fires on 'so' itself, so a degree 'so' straight after a verb and its object ('fixed it so quickly') can also fire. Quiet for ', so' after a comma, 'and so', 'so on', sentence-initial 'So', degree 'so' after is/was/felt, 'I think so', and for purpose said without 'so' ('in order to', 'lest', 'because').",
